@node Adding channels
@subsection Adding channels

Add @code{nonguix} in your channels file (@samp{$HOME/.config/guix/channels.scm}). This will later be overwritten by SSS@.

@example
(cons* (channel
      (name 'nonguix)
      (url "https://gitlab.com/nonguix/nonguix")
      (introduction
       (make-channel-introduction
        "897c1a470da759236cc11798f4e0a5f7d4d59fbc"
        (openpgp-fingerprint
         "2A39 3FFF 68F4 EF7A 3D29 12AF 6F51 20A0 22FB B2D5"))))
     %default-channels)

@end example

After adding the channel, perform a @code{guix pull} from your user account (@strong{no sudo or root}) and let all channels get updated.

@node Keeping SSS updated
@subsection Keeping SSS updated

You should every now and then update the SSS repo with @code{git pull}. You also should regularly do a @code{guix pull} from your regular user, and then rebuild this system with the root user (@code{make sr}).

Guix is a rolling distribution and you don't need to be always on bleeding edge releases, but it's nice to stay updated.

@node Initial installation of SSS from a devshell
@subsection Initial installation of SSS from a devshell

Once you have followed all those above steps, you can enter a temporary Guix shell, so as to bootstrap SSS@.

You can do this with @code{guix shell make git icecat}. Icecat will be used for you to browse temporarily and install SSS@.

Then proceed to clone SSS with @code{git clone https://codeberg.org/jjba23/sss.git} to your favourite location. This location will need to be added to @code{per-host.scm}.

You should have by now written an appropriate @code{per-host.scm} for your setup. See the above section on this topic.

Enter the directory of SSS: @code{cd sss}.

Make sure you place your @code{per-host.scm} in the root of this directory. Then you can do a @code{make sr}.

Note: @code{make sr} translates to @code{sudo guix system reconfigure config.scm --fallback}.

For more commands take a look at the SSS Makefile: @uref{../../Makefile}.

The @code{fallback} option is optional, and simply helps when upstream substitute (cache) servers are less available.

Once the system reconfigure is complete, you should also bootstrap your user's GNU home with something like: @code{guix home reconfigure ./home/joe.scm  --fallback}

You might at this point want to reset the font cache for the system and user.

@example
fc-cache -frv
sudo fc-cache -frv
@end example

You can then reboot and you should be greeted by a simple TTY@.

@node On login managers and login screens
@section On login managers, and login screens

SSS uses @strong{no login managers} like GDM or SDDM@. Simply login to the TTY and start your favorite GUI (or not).
That being said, feel free to use your own.

I like to alias my login command to @code{gui}, or sometimes I directly run @code{sway} from the TTY@.

Sometimes for the fun I work in Emacs from the TTY for that 60's and 70's computer vibe.

If you run traditional X11 sessions, you could choose to do startx instead of a Wayland session, but SSS is more geared to Wayland.

@node Nix profile
@chapter Nix profile

Nixpkgs contains a lot of software which we can leverage and manage from Guix.

SSS will automatically install and provide the Nix package manager for you.

SSS also provides some facilities to manage Nix from the comfort of your Guile Scheme.

You might need to activate and link the profile in order to be able to use, for the first time.

Assuming @code{joe} as user in question.

@code{/nix/var/nix/profiles/per-user/} should be:
@example
joe@@guixvm ~ λ  ll /nix/var/nix/profiles/per-user/

drwxr-xr-x 2 joe  users  16K 29 dec 23:09 joe/
drwxr-xr-x 2 root root  4,0K 22 nov 13:23 root/
@end example

For this, make the directory an link it to your home.

@example
sudo mkdir -p /nix/var/nix/profiles/per-user/joe
sudo chown -R joe:users /nix/var/nix/profiles/per-user/joe
ln -sfv /nix/var/nix/profiles/per-user/joe/profile $HOME/.nix-profile
@end example

It's possible you might need to logout and log back in to re-activate the profile.

You can switch your install to use nixpkgs-unstable with:

@example
nix-channel --add https://nixos.org/channels/nixpkgs-unstable
nix-channel --update
@end example

You can install Nix packages by running:

@example
nix -L profile install --impure nixpkgs#firefox
@end example

You can keep your Nix packages updated by running:

@example
NIXPGS_ALLOW_UNFREE=1 nix profile upgrade --impure '.*'
@end example

The installed software will be available, for example at: @code{$HOME/.nix-profile/bin/}

SSS provides some scripts that allow you to maintain your Nix configuration and installed packages programatically with Lisp.

You can find these scripts and the list file at @code{system/scripts} in this repo and in the Makefile.

Installing all wanted Nix packages can be done with @code{make npi} for example and updating them with @code{make npu}.

